`alarms` (#800, AC) reads `alarms.v1` as the profile's reader does: the
alarms a selector reaches, each answered "is it firing?", "is it past its
horizon?" and "does its key re-derive?", each owner judged known whole or
not, and "is anything firing at or above the floor?" over them. A judgement
verb: exit 1 on an alarm firing at or above the floor, 0 when none does and
every owner asked is known whole, 2 when an owner or an alarm is
unobservable, none was asked, or the run could not start.

```console
$ zenctl alarms --help
Read the alarms.v1 alarms a selector reaches: is anything firing?

alarms.v1's reader (spec/profiles/alarms/v1.md §2.14, §2.15), through a session in the deployment's namespace: presence and each descriptor — an owner implements alarms.v1 when its descriptor lists it, token or not (§2.13) — then S4's GET of the alarms the selector reaches, merged by the owner's stamp with what a subscription heard when --for listens; and every archive's last-known documents, which are shown, never current, and name owners a complete presence read no longer finds (absent, not asked). Per alarm, §5's questions: is it firing (a fresh firing document of the present instance; resolved, a delete, or a key its owner's alarms, known whole, do not hold, is no; stale, a loss no GET has healed, an earlier instance's put, are never yes or no); is it past its horizon, its owner present; does its key re-derive from its document. Per owner, whether its alarms are known whole: a complete GET it answered, or a complete empty one and a lossless window longer than 900 s, the owner present throughout. Over the scope, "is anything firing at or above --at-least?": yes on any alarm firing at or above it, whatever else is known; no only when the presence read that found the owners was complete, every owner asked is known whole, and none of its alarms that may be at or above it is unobservable. A reply's age needs a trusted clock: --clocks-synced is the deployment's word, or the window measures one on a live put. Exit 1 an alarm firing at or above the floor (the finding), 0 none, every owner asked known whole, 2 no verdict: an owner or an alarm unobservable, a presence read possibly incomplete, no owner asked, or a run that could not start.

Usage: zenctl alarms [OPTIONS] [SELECTOR]

Arguments:
  [SELECTOR]
          The alarms asked of: a key expression, base-relative (`zk2/…`), that reaches alarms.v1 keys — one alarm (`zk2/lab/svc/alarms.v1/state/alarms/a659f813308ad1da`), one owner's (`zk2/lab/svc/**`), a system's (`zk2/lab/**`). Omitted, every alarm in the namespace
          
          [default: zk2/*/*/alarms.v1/state/alarms/*]

Options:
      --at-least <SEVERITY>
          The floor of "is anything firing at or above it?" (alarms.v1 §2.15): info, warning or critical, in that order
          
          [default: warning]
          [possible values: info, warning, critical]

      --clocks-synced
          This host's clock and the owners' agree within the HLC delta (500 ms), as the deployment keeps them: a reply's stamp is then aged against this clock (freshness.v1 §2.6). Without it, only a clock the window measured on a live put of it is trusted

      --for <SECS>
          The reading's window, seconds: a subscription to the alarms and to the owners' instance tokens, declared before the GET, then --lease and --timeout more while the owners are watched (spec §2.7, 0.25). An owner whose GET drew nothing is known whole only by a lossless window longer than 900 s, the owner present throughout (§2.15). Omitted, no subscription: the GET alone

      --lease <SECS>
          The deployment's link lease, seconds: a window's owner is watched for this, and --timeout, past the window's end (spec §2.7, 0.25)
          
          [default: 10]

      --wait-before-close <SECS>
          The routers' wait_before_close, seconds: a window is lossless only if this reader never stalled longer than it, nor than --lease less one keep-alive (spec §2.7, 0.27)
          
          [default: 5]

      --across-face <GRANT>
          The one owner the selector names sits across a constrained face (spec §8.5), where presence and descriptors do not cross: the deployment's word that it implements alarms.v1. `granted` when the face grants this reader a GET of them (link.v1 §2.9), `denied` when it does not — then every question is unobservable, never "nothing fires" (alarms.v1 §2.11)

          Possible values:
          - granted: The face grants this reader a GET of the alarms (link.v1 §2.9)
          - denied:  It does not: every question is unobservable across it

      --namespace <NS>
          The deployment namespace: the session is opened in it.
          
          zk2 keys are base-relative (`zk2/<system>/<service>/…`), and a deployment's services run in a zenoh session namespace that prefixes them on the wire. A resolved verb opens its session in the same one, so it reads what a consumer of the deployment reads. `--base` is the same flag, and so is the active context's `base`. Resolution: flag > env > active context > empty — the bus-root deployment, no namespace. `zenctl namespace list` finds the namespaces in use.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_BASE=]
          [alias: --base]

      --context <NAME>
          Use a named context from the config file for this invocation (default: the file's `current` pointer; env `ZENCTL_CONTEXT`)

  -c, --connect <CONNECT>
          Endpoint to connect to, repeatable (e.g. `tcp/127.0.0.1:7447`).
          
          The session is a zenoh client of these endpoints: no listener of its own, no gossip, nothing the mesh can route through. An endpoint nothing answers fails the command (exit 2) rather than reading as an empty bus, and one that does not parse is refused by name (exit 2).

  -l, --listen <LISTEN>
          Endpoint to listen on, repeatable — which makes the session a peer

      --scouting
          Enable multicast scouting (off by default, and think first)

      --timeout <SECS>
          Seconds to wait for replies (default 5; a context may override the default). Bounds the presence read, each descriptor GET and each contract retrieval, a state GET, and a call — which, given none, waits what its operation recommends (`timeout_ms`)

      --zenoh-config <FILE>
          Zenoh JSON5 config file: the passthrough that reaches a secured bus.
          
          Loaded as the base layer; --connect/--listen/--scouting apply on top when given. A file that sets a session namespace is refused: the namespace is `--namespace`'s to set.
          
          [env: ZENCTL_ZENOH_CONFIG=]

      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format: table for humans, json (one document) or ndjson (one object per row) for scripts; auto = table on a tty, ndjson piped
          
          [env: ZENCTL_FORMAT=]
          [default: auto]
          [possible values: auto, table, json, ndjson]

      --color <COLOR>
          Colour: auto (a terminal gets it, a pipe does not), always, never.
          
          Colour only ever re-encodes a distinction the plain text already makes, so stripping every escape leaves the same information. `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE` are honoured under `auto`, and `--format json|ndjson` never carries an escape whatever this says.

          Possible values:
          - auto:   A terminal gets colour; a pipe does not. Honours `NO_COLOR` and `CLICOLOR_FORCE`
          - always
          - never
          
          [default: auto]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

```

A selector that reaches no alarm is refused before any session opens, exit
2, the reserved non-verdict: alarms are read in the namespace, base-relative.

```console
$ zenctl alarms acme/zk2/** --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: "acme/zk2/**" is not base-relative: alarms are read through a session in the deployment's namespace, so the selector starts at zk2/ (the namespace is --namespace)
alarms: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

```console
$ zenctl alarms zk2/lab/svc/health.v1/** --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: "zk2/lab/svc/health.v1/**" reaches no alarms.v1 key (zk2/<system>/<service>/alarms.v1/state/alarms/<alert_key>)
alarms: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```

The deployment's word for an owner across a face names one owner.

```console
$ zenctl alarms zk2/lab/** --across-face denied --zenoh-config tests/cmd/fixtures/dead-bus.json5
? 2
Error: --across-face takes the deployment's word for one owner: name its system and service in the selector (zk2/<system>/<service>/…)
alarms: the question could not be asked — exit 2, the reserved non-verdict (1 here would claim a verdict this run never reached)

```
